WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.040
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.260
Today I want to talk about mistakes.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:05.910
We all make mistakes.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:09.360
Some people are upset
when they make mistakes.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.170
I'm not, and I want to go into some
detail on how we can take advantage

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.130
of this, the mistakes that we make to
do better in our language learning.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.670
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.460
You can click on the bell
to get notifications.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.460
And by all means join me at
LingQ where I learn languages.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:34.470
So, you know, we can not understand when
things are said, or we can not understand

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.210
when we read, because we didn't quite
hear, hear it properly or we've forgotten

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.950
the word, or we don't know the word.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.760
So there are all kinds of ways
that we can simply not understand,

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.440
but that's not a mistake.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:46.970
There's no anxiety there.

00:00:47.475 --> 00:00:51.735
There's only anxiety if you don't
understand when you're in a conversation.

00:00:51.855 --> 00:00:55.095
In other words, somebody says something
to you and you don't understand what

00:00:55.095 --> 00:00:57.614
they're saying so that you can reply.

00:00:57.644 --> 00:00:58.515
You can't say anything.

00:00:58.754 --> 00:00:59.955
There there's anxiety.

00:01:00.195 --> 00:01:04.334
But if you are engaged in input
activities, such as listening and

00:01:04.334 --> 00:01:11.100
reading, certainly I can tolerate a lot
of vagueness, ambiguity, misunderstanding.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.210
It really doesn't bother me because
I know that through continuing

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:19.470
my input activities, I will
gradually see things more clearly,

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.390
understand things more clearly.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:22.890
There's no great pressure on me.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.580
However, as soon as you start speaking
with someone, things change, as I said,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.240
if you don't understand what they're
saying, that's quite intimidating.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.600
In fact, to me, that's the most
intimidating thing when I don't

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.140
understand what people are saying,
I can't even attempt to reply.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.940
However, there's also the problem when
you say something and you say it wrong,

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:51.650
you use the wrong word, you use the wrong
tense, you use the wrong form of the verb

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:57.140
or the noun, whatever it might be or, or
the structure, the phrasing is incorrect.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:58.910
And so your conscious...

00:02:00.225 --> 00:02:04.155
Or you might pronounce poorly and you're
conscious of the difficulty that you have.

00:02:04.695 --> 00:02:10.005
Uh, sometimes you can watch a video or
listen to an audio of yourself and you

00:02:10.005 --> 00:02:13.395
will notice mistakes that you made,
that maybe you weren't aware of while

00:02:13.395 --> 00:02:15.375
you were engaged in the conversation.

00:02:15.585 --> 00:02:20.025
And that's often very useful because
then you can really then zoom in on those

00:02:20.025 --> 00:02:24.015
areas and try to improve or, you know,
pay attention to them when you next come

00:02:24.015 --> 00:02:26.565
across them and in listening and reading.

00:02:27.405 --> 00:02:30.660
But, um, It is a bit unsettling.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:31.620
There's a fear.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.330
People don't want to make mistakes.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.880
Um, when we study of school, we
make mistakes on our little test.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:39.540
And so we get marked down for them.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.500
And so we, we sort of develop this
habit, this sense that mistakes are bad.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.610
Uh, if I get a test at school
and I go 10 out of 10, I'm good.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:49.950
If I go five out of 10, I'm bad.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.060
But the reality is that he's in my
experiences that I can get something right

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:57.900
one time and wrong the next time, wrong
the first time and right the next time.

00:02:58.575 --> 00:03:02.325
Uh, it depends what the
brain is doing at the moment.

00:03:02.325 --> 00:03:05.174
Sometimes it remembers it
correctly and sometimes it doesn't.

00:03:05.924 --> 00:03:09.584
And so I think it's not a good
thing to train ourselves, to

00:03:09.584 --> 00:03:11.655
think of mistakes as bad things.

00:03:12.135 --> 00:03:15.495
To me, a mistake is an
opportunity to improve.

00:03:15.885 --> 00:03:19.245
You make a mistake very often you're
aware that you made a mistake.

00:03:19.454 --> 00:03:25.035
You're either aware that, uh, you might
be aware of what the correct usage was.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.160
Or you might simply not be
sure what the correct usage is.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:36.480
And so you are bothered by that, that
being bothered by mistakes, potential

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:43.590
or actual mistakes, it drives that
curiosity, that desire to focus in

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:50.250
on that structure, that use of words
or whatever, and to get better at it.

00:03:50.924 --> 00:03:54.434
And so to that extent, mistakes
are a sign that we are pushing

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:56.295
ourselves outside of our comfort zone.

00:03:56.984 --> 00:04:02.475
Uh, it's an opportunity to become aware of
the gaps we have in what we have learned.

00:04:03.945 --> 00:04:08.415
It's a, it's a very positive thing in
the process of learning a language.

00:04:08.625 --> 00:04:10.424
That's why I'm not a fan of tests.

00:04:10.935 --> 00:04:15.195
Uh, there are people who can score
well on tests who can get certain

00:04:15.195 --> 00:04:19.155
fine points of grammar correctly,
but cannot communicate properly,

00:04:19.155 --> 00:04:20.685
don't understand very well.

00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:25.845
I think if you throw yourself into the
idea of communicating for the sake of

00:04:25.845 --> 00:04:30.765
communicating and gradually building
up your comprehension, building up your

00:04:30.765 --> 00:04:37.965
vocabulary, building up your ability to
marshal the words, uh, produce the words

00:04:37.965 --> 00:04:40.125
in the correct sort of usage pattern.

00:04:40.455 --> 00:04:42.885
All of which the brain will gradually do.

00:04:43.235 --> 00:04:46.755
The brain will not be able
to remember a specific rule.

00:04:47.355 --> 00:04:51.495
Uh, if you try to force it to
remember the, you know, the specific

00:04:51.495 --> 00:04:55.125
usage patterns, but the brain
will gradually get more and more

00:04:55.125 --> 00:05:00.105
used to these patterns, possibly
reminded by a grammatic, uh, grammar

00:05:00.105 --> 00:05:04.575
explanation, and very often reminded
by the fact that you got it wrong.

00:05:04.995 --> 00:05:09.510
And so the, the fact that you got
it wrong triggers this desire to

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.580
get closer to what that usage is.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:15.710
Now, there are people who continue to make
the same mistakes over and over again.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:20.780
And I think that's a lack of, of
attentiveness to the language.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:24.349
Uh, you have to want to notice
what's happening in the language.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:30.860
So I definitely do recommend recording,
say a conversation or doing a video

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.130
even, in a foreign language and then
listening to it and see, seeing if

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:35.480
you can pick out your own mistakes.

00:05:36.315 --> 00:05:39.435
And even if you pick them out
and you look up the rule or you

00:05:39.435 --> 00:05:42.284
pay attention to it, that doesn't
mean you're going to get it right.

00:05:42.284 --> 00:05:45.615
The next time there is
no immediate correction.

00:05:46.034 --> 00:05:48.525
Teachers may think that they
can correct you and that

00:05:48.525 --> 00:05:49.665
therefore you'll get it right.

00:05:49.784 --> 00:05:51.375
That has never been my experience.

00:05:51.555 --> 00:05:56.955
When I taught English, say, as a
tutor at LingQ, I could correct

00:05:56.955 --> 00:05:58.005
the person three, four times.

00:05:58.125 --> 00:05:59.835
They would still make the same mistake.

00:06:00.195 --> 00:06:06.270
When I go through my, uh, tutors
reports at lingQ I'll see that I get

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:10.020
the same correction from them, the
same phrase or word that I used wrong.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.850
I use it wrong week after week,
but eventually I get it right.

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:18.800
So it doesn't bother me that because my
whole, the whole learning process is a

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:25.100
continuing, a continuum of knowing less,
understanding less, making more mistakes

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:30.290
and gradually understanding or having
more words and making fewer mistakes.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:35.130
So at a given point in time,
that I'm making a lot of mistakes

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:38.430
that's an indication that I'm learning.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:43.170
You know, if I were to limit myself to
a very narrow range of words and only

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:47.250
ever talk about the weather or things
that I'm comfortable talking about,

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:51.060
where I have got a sort of a routine
that I know is more or less correct.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:53.250
That's not learning the language.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:59.460
It's only when I push myself into,
you know, ever, you know, broader,

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:01.890
uh, range of subject matter,

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:06.900
and try to express my views on
those things that I'm learning.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.650
And of course, inevitably, if I do
that, I'm going to make more mistakes.

00:07:11.039 --> 00:07:12.750
So mistakes are valuable.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:16.590
Mistakes are an indication that you're
pushing yourself beyond the comfort

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.140
of a limited range of subject matter.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:24.419
If you're paying attention to what you're
saying, paying attention to what the

00:07:24.419 --> 00:07:29.280
native speaker is saying, then you are
able to then focus in on these areas.

00:07:29.535 --> 00:07:33.195
When you are listening and reading
and all of this is part of the

00:07:33.195 --> 00:07:35.535
gradual process of improving yourself.

00:07:35.835 --> 00:07:40.395
And as I have said, often in
quoting Manfred Spitzer, the brain

00:07:40.395 --> 00:07:42.165
cannot do otherwise than learn.

00:07:42.315 --> 00:07:46.365
If you expose the brain to the patterns
of the language, you expose the brain

00:07:46.395 --> 00:07:51.285
to try to say things and getting them
wrong, and then reminding yourself

00:07:51.285 --> 00:07:52.785
of what the correct usage was,

00:07:52.965 --> 00:07:56.655
and you keep, keep on doing these
things, the brain will gradually develop.

00:07:57.045 --> 00:08:01.305
Patterns for using the language that are
more and more correct, more and more,

00:08:01.784 --> 00:08:05.625
uh, based on the standard usage of, of
what you've been listening to and what

00:08:05.625 --> 00:08:11.174
you've been hearing people say, but that
process of learning is a slow process.

00:08:11.344 --> 00:08:14.594
It's not because you are taught something
that you're going to get it right.

00:08:14.775 --> 00:08:16.155
So we need to have patience.

00:08:16.305 --> 00:08:18.765
We need to be indulgent with ourselves.

00:08:19.034 --> 00:08:24.224
We need to recognize mistakes for what
they are, valuable mind milestones

00:08:24.734 --> 00:08:26.594
as we progress in our language.

00:08:26.895 --> 00:08:27.585
So there you have it.

00:08:27.585 --> 00:08:32.475
That's my take on mistakes and, um,
I'll leave you with a couple of videos

00:08:32.475 --> 00:08:34.515
that might reinforce this message.

00:08:34.934 --> 00:08:35.924
Thanks for listening.

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:36.255
Bye.

00:08:36.284 --> 00:08:36.735
For now.

